### 1. Root & Status Check
GET http://127.0.0.1:8000/
Accept: application/json

### 2. REST API: Neuen Benutzer anlegen
POST http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/v1/users
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "username": "max_mustermann",
  "email": "max@example.com",
  "role": "lead_developer"
}

### 3. REST API: Alle Benutzer abrufen
GET http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/v1/users
Accept: application/json

### 4. REST API: Item für Benutzer 1 erstellen
POST http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/v1/items
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "title": "Dell XPS 15",
  "description": "32GB RAM, OLED Display",
  "price": 2199.00,
  "owner_id": 1
}

### 5. REST API: Benutzer nach ID abrufen (inkl. Items)
GET http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/v1/users/1
Accept: application/json

### 6. GraphQL: Benutzer über Mutation erstellen
POST http://127.0.0.1:8000/graphql
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "query": "mutation CreateUser($input: CreateUserInput!) { createUser(input: $input) { id username email role isActive } }",
  "variables": {
    "input": {
      "username": "sarah_connor",
      "email": "sarah@example.com",
      "role": "admin"
    }
  }
}

### 7. GraphQL: Benutzer mit verschachtelten Items abfragen
POST http://127.0.0.1:8000/graphql
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "query": "query { users { id username email role items { id title price } } }"
}

### 8. GraphQL: Einzelnen Benutzer abfragen
POST http://127.0.0.1:8000/graphql
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "query": "query { user(id: 1) { id username email items { title price } } }"
}

### Einfache GraphQL Abfrage: Alle Benutzer (ID & Username)
POST http://127.0.0.1:8000/graphql
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "query": "{ users { id username email } }"
}
